Probabilistic YIN from Sevag Hanssian's pitch-detection library, used by
Ikaros Studio's pitch analysis (Pitch mode in the audio editor). MIT
License (LICENSE).

  Repository   https://github.com/sevagh/pitch-detection
  Commit       8055f50bd94ffcc9fec4b63ca9a6a087eec9d892 (2025-01-07)
  Files        include/pitch_detection.h, src/yin.cpp, and the parts of
               src/autocorrelation.cpp and src/parabolic_interpolation.cpp
               that YIN uses

Only YIN and its probabilistic version are kept; MPM, the HMM (hmm.cpp) and
the tests are not. The analysis built on it (frames, notes, the cache) is
Ikaros's own: src/engine/PitchAnalysis.h.

Changed (each change is marked "Ikaros:"):
  - FFTS is replaced by juce::dsp::FFT.
  - The YIN difference function is computed exactly (the correlation of
    the frame's first half with the whole frame, zero-padded, and the
    energy of each window), where the original used a circular
    autocorrelation and an approximation of the energy terms.
  - The pitch range is fixed per instance, and periods outside it aren't
    looked at.
  - The probabilistic thresholds find their candidates with a running
    minimum and a binary search (the same candidates, much faster). Where
    no dip is below a threshold, the lowest point gets that threshold's
    weight times PYIN_PA, as pYIN describes; the original read past the end
    of its buffer there.
  - mlpack's HMM (which would bring in mlpack and Armadillo) is replaced by
    picking the most probable candidate, and the frame's voicing
    probability is the weight of the thresholds where a dip was found. The
    analysis smooths across frames itself.
  - One class for float frames instead of templates; the beta distribution
    table and the constants are unchanged.
